    "OverallSummary": "This case involves Matthew I. Turner, a General Securities Representative registered with FINRA through WestPark Capital, Inc., who engaged in excessive trading in the accounts of three customers between September 2018 and December 2020. The excessive trading violated Rule 15l-1(a)(1) under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (Reg BI) for conduct on and after June 30, 2020, FINRA Rule 2111 for conduct prior to June 30, 2020, and FINRA Rule 2010.\n\nThe Care Obligation violations were identified using two key analytical metrics:\n1. Turnover rates: The affected accounts had turnover rates of 13.01 (Customers A1 and A2's joint account) and 9.04 (Customer B's account), both well above the threshold of 6.0 that generally indicates excessive trading.\n2. Cost-to-equity ratios: The affected accounts had cost-to-equity ratios of 50.49% (Customers A1 and A2's joint account) and 27.58% (Customer B's account), both well above the 20% threshold that generally indicates excessive trading.\n\nIn total, Turner placed 72 trades in the joint account of Customers A1 (71 years old) and A2 (63 years old) between September 2018 and March 2020, generating $9,613.76 in commissions and resulting in realized losses of $115,183. For Customer B, Turner placed 110 trades between October 2019 and December 2020, generating $24,655.93 in commissions and resulting in realized losses of $45,639.\n\nAdditionally, Turner exercised discretionary authority to effect 148 trades in four customers' accounts without obtaining written authorization from the customers and without his firm having accepted the accounts as discretionary, in violation of NASD Rule 2510(b) and FINRA Rules 3260(b) and 2010.\n\nAs a result of these violations, Turner agreed to a five-month suspension from associating with any FINRA member in all capacities and to pay restitution of $27,415.75 plus interest to the affected customers."
